{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1159807-is-this-rote-rule-official","id":1159807,"title":"is this rote rule official?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-17T06:23:08-08:00","id":1159807,"title":"is this rote rule official?","content":"supposedly malcolm shepard wrote it but i can't find any evidence. anyway it goes like this:<br /> when you make a rote you can make an intelligence+ occult/science roll at difficulty 8. every 5 successes allow you to remove one dot from a normally necessary sphere and increase another one(or even add a new one) but you can't lower any sphere to less than 2 or more than 2 ranks and for each rank you change you also have to add one more sphere dot to show the relative inefficiency of doing it this way. all needs to make sense paradigmatically<br /> <br /> for instance teleporting a massive amount of people at once is correspondence 4 life 3,but if you get 5 successes you could make it correspondence 3 life 3 prime 2(or entropy 2 or mind 2 etc)<br /> TBH i like this rule and i'll use it anyway but if it's word of god ill have an easier time getting my grumpy storyteller to accept it<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T07:03:07-08:00","id":1159823,"title":"","content":"Not even remotely official and totally broken.<br /> <br /> The sphere ranks exist for a reason... specifically so teleporting massive numbers of people isn't a thing. By those rules you could reasonably get 2 successes on 8-10 dice and use master tier effects as a starting character (i.e. X sphere 5 becomes X sphere 3, Y sphere 2).<br /> <br /> All that from a system that doesn't even require XP (Rotes are free).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T07:20:53-08:00","id":1159831,"title":"","content":"It does seem to go against the spirit of the Spheres. I would like to see what's the in-game justification for that, if it's a rule. Having an in-game justification may help to convince a grumpy storyteller. For some it may be more effective than saying that some author wrote it in some forgotten manuscript.<br /> <br /> 5 successes in a normal (not extended) roll at diff 8 it's the kind of hardship that an ST puts to an action when he doesn't want you to succeed in normal circumnstances. Yet it says &quot;Every&quot; 5 successes? how many &quot;sets&quot; of 5 successes you're expected to get?, are you expected to use magick to enhance this &quot;fuck the rules of magick&quot; roll. To be a specialist at that?<br /> <br /> Personaly, I would ask you why you think the aditional Spheres can &quot;help&quot; you in a way that the Rote would require less expertice in another Sphere. Then allow you to do it without rolling If you convince me. The roll seems an unnecesary way of limiting your success, that's very much begging the player to find ways to rigg it (lower difficulties, increase Intelligence temporarily), and ultimately serves the only purpose of guilt trip the ST into accepting whatever bullshit sphere you want to use because &quot;you succeeded at the roll&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 11-17-2017, 10:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T08:26:17-08:00","id":1159861,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1159807-is-this-rote-rule-official?p=1159831#post1159831\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It does seem to go against the spirit of the Spheres. I would like to see what's the in-game justification for that, if it's a rule. Having an in-game justification may help to convince a grumpy storyteller. For some it may be more effective than saying that some author wrote it in some forgotten manuscript.<br /> <br /> 5 successes in a normal (not extended) roll at diff 8 it's the kind of hardship that an ST puts to an action when he doesn't want you to succeed in normal circumnstances. Yet it says &quot;Every&quot; 5 successes? how many &quot;sets&quot; of 5 successes you're expected to get?, are you expected to use magick to enhance this &quot;fuck the rules of magick&quot; roll. To be a specialist at that?<br /> <br /> Personaly, I would ask you why you think the aditional Spheres can &quot;help&quot; you in a way that the Rote would require less expertice in another Sphere. Then allow you to do it without rolling If you convince me. The roll seems an unnecesary way of limiting your success, that's very much begging the player to find ways to rigg it (lower difficulties, increase Intelligence temporarily), and ultimately serves the only purpose of guilt trip the ST into accepting whatever bullshit sphere you want to use because &quot;you succeeded at the roll&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> keep in mind that non pcs can make rotes too. even without lowering the difficulty, a 5000 year old archmaster from atlantis or something with intelligence and occult at 10 could get 10,15, or even all 20 successes if he had nothing better to do every day of the last decade again, this never appeared in a book, was something i found on a site which supposedly quotes malcolm,(fortunately i found it again, (<a href=\"http://de.magus.wikia.com/wiki/Quotes_from_Malcolm_Sheppard\" target=\"_blank\">http://de.magus.wikia.com/wiki/Quote...lcolm_Sheppard</a>) ) anyway the idea was to show that the sphere system is not some cosmic absolute .<br /> we've all seen rotes that as written do not make much sense yet they work because the mage making it says they do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T10:43:09-08:00","id":1159914,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">we've all seen rotes that as written do not make much sense yet they work because the mage making it says they do.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're mixing IOC explanations with OOC rules, that's the path to the dark side<br /> <br /> Rotes never say: &quot;This shouldn't work RAW, but it does because the mage says so&quot;, they just give a number of Spheres that the author tought it made sense, but us as readers think it doesn't. Even then, this doesn't explain half of the oddities of Rotes: Rotes almost never require less of a Sphere than what's usually needed, it's much more common for them to ignore or alter the results of the success table (which isn't explained in the link).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> keep in mind that non pcs can make rotes too. even without lowering the difficulty, a 5000 year old archmaster from atlantis or something with intelligence and occult at 10 could get 10,15, or even all 20 successes if he had nothing better to do every day of the last decade again, this never appeared in a book, </div> </div> </div>So <i>Malcom</i> was thinking in the beneffit of a 5000 years PC that use rules of Archmagic and then another set of optional rules to raise traits without Paradox...<br /> <br /> Don't you think it's more likely that it will be abused by standard Mage powergamers that notice how easy it's to reduce difficulties in any roll and that you can raise mental traits with Mind 1...and then get to use Sphere 5 effects for &quot;free&quot;?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> anyway the idea was to show that the sphere system is not some cosmic absolute . </div> </div> </div>The link you given does offer a justification, which implies one has to be given: <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"> You justify it by saying</span></span></b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\">...X</span></span><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\">. </span></span></b>As per the link, the rote doesn't allow to reduce cost for no reason, a reason was given to someone, I think this shouldn't be pushed to the side. This is exactly what I was saying with the difference that I proposed to not require any roll because, and I quote:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> The roll seems an unnecesary way of limiting your success,<i> that's very much begging the player to find ways to rigg it (lower difficulties, increase Intelligence temporarily)</i>,<i>and ultimately serves the only purpose of guilt trip the ST into accepting whatever bullshit sphere you want to use because &quot;you succeeded at the roll&quot;</i><b>. </b></div> </div> </div> </div>I think I've found my least favorite rule in all of Mage, good it was never printed. I'm all about Rotes breaking the rules, but this isn't the way to do it.<br /> <br /> It's not necessarily gamebreaking if we stress that a IOC reason has to be given, and if we limmit the posibility of affecting the result with more magick, but the spirit of the rule it's all wrong, a roll shouldn't be needed to ask the aproval of the ST. Much less to avoid having to ask for said aproval<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">was something i found on a site which supposedly quotes malcolm</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar164_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"164\" data-vbnamecard=\"164\">Malcolm</a>: Do you have anything to say about all of this? .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 11-17-2017, 01:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-17T12:43:12-08:00","id":1159974,"title":"","content":"&quot;Don't you think it's more likely that it will be abused by standard Mage powergamers that notice how easy it's to reduce difficulties in any roll and that you can raise mental traits with Mind 1...and then get to use Sphere 5 effects for &quot;free&quot;?&quot;<br /> <br /> yeah,good point. it's kinda funny how whitewolf writers of all gamelines always answer this by saying &quot;uhm we don't minmax so it didn't occur to us<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />&quot;<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-20T20:08:26-08:00","id":1161057,"title":"","content":"I can see an in-game justification for this. It's just figuring out different ways to accomplish the same effect. As a simple example, lighting a candle usually uses something like Forces 2, presumably to heat up the wick enough to cause it to ignite. However, couldn't someone try using Matter to say, break apart the molecules in the paraffin, thereby causing an exothermic reaction? One might say that then yeah, they can just go ahead and do that, but that was the player doing the Science roll there instead of the character. And since usually characters are better at Science (or Occult) than players, that's what the roll is for, so the player doesn't have to come up with an in-game explanation that probably requires knowledge they don't have, just like any ability roll.<br /> <br /> As for play balance, I don't see it as being able to 'get around the rules for free', instead it is rewarding those who are intelligent and knowledgeable, as well as those willing to put in the time and effort to figure out an alternative method. Think of it like MacGyver; if he doesn't have some piece of equipment he needs, he can usually build it out of what's around him (if there's enough other things of course). Not many people can do that. So instead of looking at it like powergaming, its more like simply being smart and hardworking. Even the inefficiency rule is true to life (often MacGyver's inventions don't work as well as the real thing would), and is another &quot;cost&quot; needing to be paid for this approach. If you had the right spheres to begin with, it would both work better, and you wouldn't need to put in all that time and effort (as well as risk since you may not find another way) into researching an alternative approach.<br /> <br /> And while it may not be in the spirit of &quot;play balance&quot;, magic is a largely mental activity, so smarter people are going to do better at it in general. If they've figured out a way to affect that with magic, then yes, that's a big deal, but entirely realistic. The only way around that is to not allow that kind of magic to begin with.<br /> <br /> As for the needing of lots of successes, often Science (and other research/development type) rolls can be cooperative, and also are often extended. Groups of scientists/occultists/programmers/etc. working together for a long time to solve very difficult problems. This could be just like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/20880-murdoc","name":"Murdoc","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar20880_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T07:12:52-08:00","id":1161509,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">yeah,good point. it's kinda funny how whitewolf writers of all gamelines always answer this by saying &quot;uhm we don't minmax so it didn't occur to us<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, WoD writters are defensive to default, however I stated that <u>this rule isn't necessarily OP</u>. It's problems go way above and beyond that. The rule strips the ST of the most basic right of deciding what Spheres do you need to cast a Rote: Instead a roll (that can fail or not) takes care of that. This isn't like a Counterspelling that may be a little biased towards other splats, this rule it's flawed by design. What it tries to accomplish is entirely unnecessary.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Murdoc</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can see an in-game justification for this. It's just figuring out different ways to accomplish the same effect. As a simple example, lighting a candle usually uses something like Forces 2, presumably to heat up the wick enough to cause it to ignite. However, couldn't someone try using Matter to say, break apart the molecules in the paraffin, thereby causing an exothermic reaction? One might say that then yeah, they can just go ahead and do that, but that was the player doing the Science roll there instead of the character.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course there are different ways to accomplish the same effect...Sphere system it's built around this idea. You can use Entropy 4 to cast a &quot;death curse&quot; or Life 3, neither it's better or worse (well, Entropy 4 it's a little worse, but that's a defect of the system). If you find a different way to accomplish an effect, you ask the ST if this is right and what Sphere leve do you need. For instance, I could say that breaking appart the molecules of the candle it's Matter 3/Forces 1 (destroy matter in a way that produces forces).<br /> <br /> With this rule it would be Forces 1/Matter 2 (or Forces 0 , Matter 2, Bullshit Sphere at 1, if the player so decides). It can't be Matter 3 because you can't increase ranks more than 2. Forcing the ST to accept a spread isn't in the spirit of the rules. This rule puts very specific constraints to what you can do with the Spheres, it doesn't make them more flexible.<br /> <br /> And if the ST doesn't agree in &quot;changing the rules&quot; for your Paradigm, I don't see why should agree to use something that was found in a Wiky and hasn't any support in canon material. This it's rule lawyering of the worst kind (this isn't an obscure data from the books, this is an obscure data that could have gotten into the books), and many ST would find that offensive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And since usually characters are better at Science (or Occult) than players, that's what the roll is for, so the player doesn't have to come up with an in-game explanation that probably requires knowledge they don't have, just like any ability roll. </div> </div> </div>But the player has to come &quot;with an in-game explanation &quot; because, I quote from the rule:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcom?</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">...You also need to justify it in paradigm...</div> </div> </div> </div>Now, I'm alright with this, but then don't ask me to roll.<br /> <br /> This gives Intelligent characters that know Science/Occult and advantage on magick disregarding that there are magical paradigms that don't rely on intelligence (or on those skills). This can be fix by being more flexible with the requirements of the roll (Unlike the whole &quot;naa, the ST can't decide that you can use other sphere to do the same effect&quot; thing, that can't be fixed)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for play balance, I don't see it as being able to 'get around the rules for free', instead it is rewarding those who are intelligent and knowledgeable, as well as those willing to put in the time and effort to figure out an alternative method. Think of it like MacGyver; if he doesn't have some piece of equipment he needs, he can usually build it out of what's around him (if there's enough other things of course). Not many people can do that. So instead of looking at it like powergaming, its more like simply being smart and hardworking. Even the inefficiency rule is true to life (often MacGyver's inventions don't work as well as the real thing would), and is another &quot;cost&quot; needing to be paid for this approach. If you had the right spheres to begin with, it would both work better, and you wouldn't need to put in all that time and effort (as well as risk since you may not find another way) into researching an alternative approach.<br /> <br /> And while it may not be in the spirit of &quot;play balance&quot;, magic is a largely mental activity, so smarter people are going to do better at it in general. If they've figured out a way to affect that with magic, then yes, that's a big deal, but entirely realistic. The only way around that is to not allow that kind of magic to begin with.<br /> <br /> As for the needing of lots of successes, often Science (and other research/development type) rolls can be cooperative, and also are often extended. Groups of scientists/occultists/programmers/etc. working together for a long time to solve very difficult problems. This could be just like that. </div> </div> </div>Since the roll it's extended by default, that's not really such a problem as I thought when Mark presented the rule. It does make the rule more OP. However:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcom?</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>You must have both the Sphere ranks of the base Effect and those of the rote requirements.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>So you can't really beneffit from it, it's for the beneffit of others only. It doesn't allow you to &quot;McGwiver&quot; anything. You need both the regular Spheres needed for the effect and the &quot;new&quot; ones<br /> <br /> Ok, powergamers ain't going to like this rule a whole lot, but that wasn't my major issue.<br /> <br /> This rule doesn't explain the oddities of Rotes (which often makes them more efficient than the regular rules) nor allows you to replicate them. It places arbitrary, but strong, limmits on what you can accomplish at a given sphere level. It tries to &quot;force&quot; the ST to accept said arbitrary spread.<br /> <br /> I don't see the value of a rule that addresses nothing, avoids nothing and has mechanical imbalances and weirdness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 11-22-2017, 10:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-20T23:36:36-08:00","id":1188086,"title":"","content":"1) My name has two Ls and two Ps.<br /> <br /> 2) That was a prototype rule I meant to further tweak and find a place for in order to give rotes some heft. Certainly you'd always need ST permission and in-paradigm justification.<br /> <br /> 3) The idea that the Spheres are absolute, eternal governors of how willworking functions is something *characters* may believe, but *players* should not. The nature of Awakened magic/k has changed at least once in its history, and probably changed once before. Phil may now work with a line that the Spheres are eternal (and that's his right, absolutely), but this wasn't the case in the early 2000s.<br /> <br /> 4) In game terms, we're talking about a maximum 2 point difficulty break and the ability to learn a single constricted Effect more easily. Where it can get serious is where the 2 point break stacks with other modifiers, but in MRev you could knock almost anything down to difficulty 3 quickly enough. Where it also gets interesting is in its teachability, which means you can make your apprentices very good at a few tricks. Teachability and the use of grimoires would have been part of any full treatment.<br /> <br /> 5) In execution it would accompany a discussion of how, while this is the current state of the art for modifying the Effect/Sphere relationship, like the Spheres itself, this is probably not the *only* way, and various oddities in how many rotes are constructed may come from other forgotten or never-duplicated methods.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T07:09:10-08:00","id":1188163,"title":"","content":"\u200bThanks for answering, your name was thrown left and right here, hence I tought it was reasonable to call you (since you're part of the comunity).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1159807-is-this-rote-rule-official?p=1188086#post1188086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) My name has two Ls and two Ps.<br /> <br /> 2) That was a prototype rule I meant to further tweak and find a place for in order to give rotes some heft. Certainly you'd always need ST permission and in-paradigm justification.</div> </div> </div> </div>Glad to hear.<br /> <br /> Revised &quot;rules&quot; of Rotes stated in no uncertain terms that they sometimes don't follow regular Sphere rules. I kinda like that small &quot;anti-rule&quot;. I would use such a rule to explain many ways in which rotes differ from the general rules, like when a rote causes more damage than normal or last less than normal, or when it uses other spheres - To craft a <b>hard </b>rule about all of that seems...dificult. Maybe with a little polish I could see such a system as an alternate to Pillars, mini-spheres and alternate spheres (all of whom I wholy aprove).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3) The idea that the Spheres are absolute, eternal governors of how willworking functions is something *characters* may believe, but *players* should not. The nature of Awakened magic/k has changed at least once in its history, and probably changed once before. Phil may now work with a line that the Spheres are eternal (and that's his right, absolutely), but this wasn't the case in the early 2000s.<br /> <br /> 4) In game terms, we're talking about a maximum 2 point difficulty break and the ability to learn a single constricted Effect more easily. Where it can get serious is where the 2 point break stacks with other modifiers, but in MRev you could knock almost anything down to difficulty 3 quickly enough. Where it also gets interesting is in its teachability, which means you can make your apprentices very good at a few tricks. Teachability and the use of grimoires would have been part of any full treatment.<br /> <br /> 5) In execution it would accompany a discussion of how, while this is the current state of the art for modifying the Effect/Sphere relationship, like the Spheres itself, this is probably not the *only* way, and various oddities in how many rotes are constructed may come from other forgotten or never-duplicated methods </div> </div> </div>Phil stated that he doesn't like Characters talking about or using Spheres <i>in game</i>. I agree. But we're talikg of mechainics here, dots and dice - I believe that, even if mages do know about the Spheres as a sort of magical esperanto, they don't know about dots and dice (albeit if any splat is going to break the fourth wall, it has to be mages <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ). As a mechanic, it's nonsense to say that Spheres amount to *any* kind of *truth*. A mechanic hasn't truth value, it has a strictly *utilitarian* value<br /> <br /> Now, I don't like mages fiddling with Sphere levels to &quot;make this rote easier/harder to learn&quot;, like they know about dots and XP costs. I want my mages as far from the System as I can get them. I can see Paradigms coupled with long research discovering easier ways to do stuff (which mechanically can be represented many ways) but I don't see mages purposely gaming the &quot;enlightenment&quot; needed to cast something to provide apprentices with an advantage.<br /> <br /> If I wanted mages to be able to learn constrained Rotes outside the normal system (to represent the advantages of having a Mentor, Grimoire or a Tradition that strongly focuses on some spells that are normaly very hard - like, say, shapeshifter shamans or exorcists), I would invent a system so they can do just that - learn constrained Rotes at lower Arete/Sphere levels - instead of changing the way Spheres relate to feats. It's a Rote feature, not a Sphere feature, there's no need to alter the whole Sphere System. IOC we justify it saying that it's easier to learn a static, formulaic, trick than the whole of truth (or something like that). That way we don't have mages thinking &quot;What Spheres can I add to this Adept level Rote to make it Apprentice level?&quot; but rather &quot;What Rotes can I invest into my Grimoire, knowing that perhaps an Apprentice can learn them?&quot; - the difference between these two being that the first one actually aknowledges the Spheres as an absolute governor (that can be cheated, and thus exist) while the other merely aknowledges the fact that Apprentices can learn potent rotes under special conditions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 02-21-2018, 10:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T13:48:46-08:00","id":1188291,"title":"","content":"I like to point out that to invent a rote this way you must have the full sphere ratings to pull the effect of.<br /> So starting level chars can only cast rank 3 effects, unless the storyteller gives them the altered rote(mentor/library/ally/etc.)<br /> <br /> By the way I think the quote is from shadownessence<br /> I remember reading about that years ago.<br /> <br /> Oops <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> already pointed that out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":12022,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","user":"Nonsense","date":". 02-21-2018, 06:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-21T19:46:25-08:00","id":1188380,"title":"","content":"Figuring out how to build effects is difficult enough without rotes being valid examples of how to use the Sphere system. Then again, I've scratched my head at nearly every published rote, especially the ones that require a ton of prime for no apparent reason.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: the Ascension - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1191028-redesigned-prime-sphere\" target=\"_blank\">Redesigned Prime Sphere</a>; <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1188769-simpler-wonder-creation\" target=\"_blank\">Streamlined Wonder Creation</a><br /> Mummy: the Curse - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/474927-mummy-the-curse-2e-conversion\" target=\"_blank\">Lightweight 2E Conversion</a>; <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/22378-second-sight-tradition-disciples-of-duat\" target=\"_blank\">Disciples of Duat</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/61-octavo","name":"Octavo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar61_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1171"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-22T05:06:04-08:00","id":1188466,"title":"","content":"I take them as curious precedents rather than law. They demonstrate how to <i>not </i>follow the Sphere system (which, to me, it's in itself useful).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}